   "about": "Assessment of Japan’s remaining textile capacity, its prewar and wartime organization, and measures for post-surrender recovery.",
   "summary": "The report estimates that surviving cotton and synthetic fiber facilities could meet a minimum level of Japanese civilian demand if raw materials were available. It describes how major firms dominated prewar textile cartels, gained greater control through wartime production and distribution bodies, and acquired or displaced smaller producers as plants and equipment shifted to war work. It recommends inventories, imports, repairs and reconversion, allocating initial civilian supplies at the 1943 level, abolishing wartime control structures, and supporting independent firms under a new Textile Control Commission.",
